    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 18th with a high-ceiling outlook, led by **Yordan Alvarez** (75.62) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (73.09). **Yordan Alvarez** is our premier play, capitalizing on an elite matchup against Rojas with an xERA of 5.83, where his xwOBA of .425 should neutralize the minor platoon penalty. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** earns his high efficiency score through favorable wind conditions and a dynamic platoon advantage against Sproat, effectively mitigating his disadvantageous #8 spot in the order. **Trea Turner** (70.06) and **Colt Keith** (69.13) remain essential anchors, with **Trea Turner** benefiting significantly from wind-assisted output and top-of-the-order protection, while **Colt Keith** exploits a slow-delivery pitcher to maximize his stolen base potential, a critical component for our category weightings.\n\nRegarding bench strategy, **Christian Yelich** (53.20) was the closest inclusion to the starting lineup, ultimately making the cut despite a sharp drop-off due to his platoon disadvantage against Imanaga. **Junior Caminero** (58.15) holds onto his spot by a thread, as the park factors and platoon splits were less favorable than expected, pushing him into the high-marginal category. We continue to monitor the status of **Lars Nootbaar** and **Zac Veen**, both of whom remain unavailable, forcing us to rely on our active core. With **Dillon Dingler** (48.45) and **Hyeseong Kim** (47.68) sitting near our floor threshold, we must be prepared to pivot to the bench if these matchups deteriorate mid-game. We are currently cycling through depth options like **Chase Meidroth**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Luke Raley**, **Josh Smith**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Luis Pena** to ensure we hit our daily efficiency targets without exhausting our roster caps, keeping a close eye on the weather flags and late-breaking lineup changes for the back end of the order.",
